WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.620
A Long parameter named amountMinor will take an order id without complaint.

2
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.097
Both are Long, so the compiler can't tell them apart.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.895
The classic fix is a small wrapper class.

4
00:00:13.895 --> 00:00:17.820
It works, but every amount is now an extra object on the heap.

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:24.648
Kotlin's value class keeps the type safety, and wherever it can, it drops the object.

6
00:00:24.648 --> 00:00:28.917
You write @JvmInline value class, with exactly one property.

7
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:31.644
To the compiler, Cents is its own type.

8
00:00:31.644 --> 00:00:35.248
Hand charge a plain Long, and the call does not compile.

9
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.304
At run time, most of it is gone.

10
00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:42.779
Here is the Kotlin, next to what javap shows the compiler built.

11
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.868
charge takes a plain long, under a name with a hash on the end.

12
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.818
Cents(150) calls a static method, constructor-impl.

13
00:00:50.818 --> 00:00:54.683
It runs your init block and hands back the same long.

14
00:00:54.683 --> 00:00:56.214
No object is created.

15
00:00:56.214 --> 00:00:59.713
plus became a static method that adds two longs.

16
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:03.142
So this whole call runs on plain longs.

17
00:01:03.142 --> 00:01:05.131
No Cents object is ever made.

18
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:07.584
So when is a box needed?

19
00:01:07.584 --> 00:01:11.643
A value class is boxed whenever it is used as another type.

20
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:14.357
Here is a second value class, Amount.

21
00:01:14.357 --> 00:01:16.744
It implements an interface, Priced.

22
00:01:16.744 --> 00:01:18.040
Take one amount, a.

23
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:22.656
In an Invoice, it is used as an Amount, so the field is a plain long.

24
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:25.857
Pass it to asGeneric, and it is used as a T.

25
00:01:25.857 --> 00:01:31.499
So the compiler calls box-impl first, and a real Amount object appears on the heap.

26
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.056
A nullable Amount has to be able to hold null, and a long can't.

27
00:01:36.056 --> 00:01:37.281
So that boxes too.

28
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:40.154
Used as the interface Priced, it boxes.

29
00:01:40.154 --> 00:01:44.840
Used as Any, it boxes, and that covers every collection and println.

30
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:49.876
Here are the same calls as Java, read back from the bytecode.

31
00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:55.161
Each time, Amount.box-impl runs right before the call that needs the object.

32
00:01:55.367 --> 00:02:00.162
It is the same rule Java uses for the primitive long and the boxed Long.

33
00:02:00.162 --> 00:02:03.892
So a List of Amount holds one Amount object per element.

34
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:05.610
Now that name.

35
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:10.192
The compiler adds a hash to every function that takes a value class.

36
00:02:10.192 --> 00:02:14.841
A dash can't appear in a Java method name, so Java can't call charge.

37
00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:18.001
@JvmName gives it a name Java can call.

38
00:02:18.001 --> 00:02:24.775
But Java passes a raw long, so the init block never runs, and minus 5 cents gets charged.
